ML_PAGE("l5-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l5\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 5 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain, apply to the scenario, and justify. The model answers below show the standard to aim for; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l5\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>Identify → Explain → Justify → Evaluate</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Identify the problem</b><p>Different numerical magnitudes (scaling) or unnecessary dimensions (selection)?</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Which statistics the scaler uses, or how the selector judges usefulness.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Justify the choice</b><p>Tie it to the algorithm (distance? thresholds?) and the data (outliers? 10,000 features?).</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate</b><p>Limitations, leakage, and how you would validate the result.</p></div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Use RobustScaler because it handles outliers.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">A conclusion with no mechanism, no limitation and no validation.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"RobustScaler is appropriate to consider because the income feature contains extreme values that distort the mean, standard deviation, minimum and maximum. It centres each value on the median and divides by the interquartile range, statistics that are far less sensitive to outliers, so ordinary customers are not compressed by a few extreme incomes. The outliers themselves remain in the data, and the choice should still be checked against validation performance.\"</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Why is feature scaling important for KNN but generally unnecessary for Random Forest? (5 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l5-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l5-1\">KNN classifies by measuring numerical distances between observations, so a feature with a large range - income in tens of thousands of ringgit - dominates the Euclidean distance even if it is not more predictive than age or experience. Scaling puts the features on comparable scales so similarity is not decided by units. A Random Forest instead consists of decision trees that split one feature at a time on a threshold; rescaling a feature simply moves its threshold, so the splits and predictions are essentially unchanged and scaling is usually unnecessary.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Compare StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler and RobustScaler for data with outliers. (8 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l5-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l5-2\">StandardScaler computes $z=(x-\\mu)/\\sigma$, giving the training feature mean 0 and standard deviation 1; it is a common default for scale-sensitive models but its mean and standard deviation are pulled by extreme values. MinMaxScaler computes $(x-x_{\\min})/(x_{\\max}-x_{\\min})$, mapping the training range to [0, 1]; because the extremes define the range, one outlier compresses all ordinary values - in the lecture's video data, 1,295 views becomes only 0.068. RobustScaler uses $(x-\\text{median})/IQR$, statistics that resist outliers, so ordinary values keep a readable spread. None of them removes outliers, all are fitted on training data only, and the final choice should be validated.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain one limitation of variance-based feature selection. (4 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l5-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l5-3\">VarianceThreshold removes features whose variance falls below a chosen threshold, which efficiently eliminates constant or near-constant columns. However, it is unsupervised: it never examines the relationship between a feature and the target. A rare binary flag present in 1% of transactions has variance $0.01\\times0.99=0.0099$ and would be removed at threshold 0.15, even if it is highly predictive of fraud. Low variance therefore does not mean low predictive relevance.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Compare filter, wrapper and embedded feature-selection methods. (6 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l5-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l5-4\">Filter methods (thresholds, VarianceThreshold, chi-square, ANOVA, mutual information) score features with statistics without repeatedly fitting the final model; they are fast and good for initial screening but univariate filters can miss interactions and redundancy. Wrapper methods such as RFE repeatedly fit a model, rank features and eliminate the weakest, so selection reflects the model and the other retained features, at a high computational cost. Embedded methods such as Random Forest importance obtain relevance during the model's own training, at moderate cost, but the importances depend on that model and are not causal. A sensible workflow filters first, then applies a wrapper or embedded method to the smaller candidate set.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EVALUATE</span>\n        <h4>30 features give 95.09% accuracy; 15 selected features give 95.26%. Evaluate. (6 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l5-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l5-5\">Halving the feature set produced a slight reported increase of 0.17 percentage points, showing that keeping every feature is not necessarily beneficial: some are redundant or noisy, and the smaller model is cheaper and easier to explain. However, 0.17 points is a very small difference that could arise from sampling variation, so it does not on its own prove better generalization; repeated evaluation would be needed. Selecting the features on the full dataset before cross-validation also leaks information between folds - selection must be fitted inside each training fold.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">SCENARIO</span>\n        <h4>A factory predicts machine failure from 200+ sensors: large ranges, constant sensors, correlated sensors. Propose a strategy. (10 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l5-6\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l5-6\">The target (Failure / No failure) makes this supervised classification. First split the data. Because sensor ranges differ greatly, scale the features if the model is distance- or gradient-based - StandardScaler for well-behaved distributions, RobustScaler where valid extreme readings distort the mean and standard deviation. Remove constant sensors with VarianceThreshold, but do not discard rare low-variance signals blindly, since an abnormal reading may indicate failure. Screen remaining features against the failure label with ANOVA or mutual information, then use RFE or Random Forest importance to handle correlated, redundant sensors within a chosen model. Fit every scaling and selection step on the training data only (ideally in a Pipeline inside cross-validation) and choose the final subset by validation performance and computational cost.</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Practice questions and worked answers from the notes</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P1</span><span>How to answer subjective questions</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>The best approach for most 5-10 mark questions is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nIdentify\n\\rightarrow Explain\n\\rightarrow Justify\n\\rightarrow Evaluate\n}$$</div>\n<p>For example, suppose the examiner asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A bank has customer-income data containing extreme outliers. Recommend an appropriate feature-scaling method.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use RobustScaler because it handles outliers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer would be:</p>\n<p>RobustScaler is appropriate to consider because the dataset contains extreme income values that can distort statistics such as the mean, standard deviation, minimum and maximum. RobustScaler transforms numerical features using the median and interquartile range, which are less sensitive to outliers. Consequently, ordinary income observations are less likely to become compressed by a small number of extreme values. However, the outliers remain in the dataset, and the method should still be assessed using the model's validation performance.</p>\n<p>Notice the difference: the stronger answer explains what, why, how and limitations.</p>\n<p>That is the standard we'll aim for.</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P2</span><span>Full Subjective Scenario</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><h4>30. A factory predicts machine failure</h4>\n<p>Suppose the examiner asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A manufacturing company wants to predict equipment failure using temperature, pressure, vibration, machine age and 200 additional sensor variables. Several sensors have large numerical ranges, some record constant values, and others are highly correlated. Propose an appropriate feature-scaling and selection strategy. (10 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The best approach is to reason through the problem systematically.</p>\n<h5>Step 1 - Identify the objective</h5>\n<p>The task is to predict machine failure.</p>\n<p>If the target is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Failure / No Failure\n</code></pre>\n<p>it is supervised classification.</p>\n<h5>Step 2 - Identify whether scaling is needed</h5>\n<p>Some sensors have very different numerical magnitudes.</p>\n<p>If a distance-based or scale-sensitive classifier is used, these differences may distort its calculations.</p>\n<p>Scaling is therefore appropriate.</p>\n<h5>Step 3 - Choose a scaler</h5>\n<p>Inspect the sensor distributions.</p>\n<p>If there are no major outliers, StandardScaler may be suitable.</p>\n<p>If extreme valid sensor readings substantially distort mean and standard deviation, RobustScaler may be preferable.</p>\n<p>The choice should depend on the data distribution and model.</p>\n<h5>Step 4 - Remove constant features</h5>\n<p>Some sensors always record the same value.</p>\n<p>VarianceThreshold can identify and remove such features because they do not vary across the training observations.</p>\n<h5>Step 5 - Evaluate predictive relevance</h5>\n<p>Statistical methods such as ANOVA or mutual information can help screen features against the failure target.</p>\n<p>But don't assume that a low-variance feature is useless - a rare abnormal sensor signal could be highly predictive of failure.</p>\n<h5>Step 6 - Consider redundancy</h5>\n<p>Some sensors measure similar physical quantities and may be highly correlated.</p>\n<p>A further selection method, such as RFE or Random Forest importance, can help assess which features contribute useful information within a particular estimator.</p>\n<h5>Step 7 - Prevent leakage and evaluate</h5>\n<p>All scaling and selection rules must be fitted on the training data.</p>\n<p>Then assess the selected-feature model using held-out or cross-validation data.</p>\n<p><strong>Complete model answer</strong></p>\n<p>The company should first examine its numerical sensor features and their distributions. Since the variables have substantially different numerical ranges, feature scaling may be necessary, particularly if the selected algorithm depends on distance or numerical optimization. StandardScaler may be suitable for reasonably distributed features, whereas RobustScaler is preferable to consider when extreme sensor readings distort mean-based statistics. Feature selection should then address unnecessary dimensions. VarianceThreshold can remove constant or near-constant sensor variables, while supervised statistical tests can assess associations between sensor features and the known failure labels. Additional model-based techniques such as RFE or Random Forest importance may help identify redundant or less informative variables. However, rare low-variance signals should not be removed blindly because they may indicate abnormal machine conditions. To ensure reliable evaluation, all transformations must be learned from the training data and applied consistently to validation and test observations. The final feature subset should be selected based on predictive performance, computational efficiency and its ability to generalize.</p>\n<p>This answer incorporates application, analysis and evaluation, which are higher levels of Bloom's taxonomy.</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P3</span><span>Another Important Scenario</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><h4>31. A dataset has 500 features but only 100 observations</h4>\n<p>Suppose the examiner asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Discuss why feature selection may be necessary and recommend an approach. (8 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should immediately recognize a potential dimensionality problem.</p>\n<p>There are more features than observations.</p>\n<p>This can increase overfitting risk, particularly when many features contain noise or are redundant.</p>\n<p>But don't simply say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Remove 400 features.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That would be arbitrary.</p>\n<p>A better approach:</p>\n<p>Feature selection may be useful because the dataset has a high number of variables relative to its sample size, increasing the risk of fitting noise rather than generalizable patterns. An initial filter method can remove constant variables and screen out irrelevant features efficiently. If the task is supervised, statistical scores can help identify candidate features associated with the target. A wrapper or embedded method may then be used on a smaller candidate set to evaluate feature usefulness in the context of the chosen model. However, the number of retained features should be selected through appropriate validation rather than an arbitrary cutoff, and all selection operations must occur within the training procedure to avoid leakage.</p>\n<p>Notice how the answer doesn't merely name methods.</p>\n<p>It explains why the sequence makes sense.</p></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P4</span><span>Likely Subjective Questions</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>These are the questions I would prepare most seriously.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What your answer must include</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain feature scaling and why it matters</td>\n<td>Different numerical ranges, distance dominance, algorithm sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler and RobustScaler</td>\n<td>Formulas, reference statistics, outlier behavior, suitability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain the curse of dimensionality</td>\n<td>High-dimensional sparsity, overfitting risk, computational cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Differentiate scaling, selection and extraction</td>\n<td>What changes and what remains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain VarianceThreshold and its limitations</td>\n<td>Variance, unsupervised nature, rare predictive features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare Chi-Square, ANOVA and Mutual Information</td>\n<td>Their statistical relationships with targets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain SelectKBest</td>\n<td>Rank features, keep highest K, supervised scoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain how RFE works</td>\n<td>Fit, rank, eliminate, repeat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare filter, wrapper and embedded methods</td>\n<td>Mechanisms, advantages, limitations, trade-offs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain Random Forest feature importance</td>\n<td>Predictive importance, model dependence, noncausality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discuss feature selection using the breast-cancer benchmark</td>\n<td>30 vs 15, accuracy difference, limitations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain preprocessing leakage and pipelines</td>\n<td>Train-only fitting, validation/test consistency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l5\" id=\"l5s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">22 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 5 - Subjective Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s0\" title=\"Lecture 5 - Subjective Exam Preparation\">Subjective overview</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 1 - How to answer subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s1\" title=\"Part 1 - How to answer subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>How to answer</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - Feature Scaling\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s2\" title=\"Part 2 - Feature Scaling\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Why scaling matters</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - StandardScaler\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s3\" title=\"Part 3 - StandardScaler\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>StandardScaler</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - MinMaxScaler\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s4\" title=\"Part 4 - MinMaxScaler\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>MinMaxScaler</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - RobustScaler\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s5\" title=\"Part 5 - RobustScaler\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>RobustScaler</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Comparing the three scalers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s6\" title=\"Part 6 - Comparing the three scalers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Comparing scalers</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - Feature Selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s7\" title=\"Part 7 - Feature Selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>What feature selection is</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - Curse of Dimensionality\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s8\" title=\"Part 8 - Curse of Dimensionality\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Curse of dimensionality</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - The three feature-selection approaches\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s9\" title=\"Part 9 - The three feature-selection approaches\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Three selection families</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - Filter Methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s10\" title=\"Part 10 - Filter Methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Filter methods</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - Supervised Statistical Selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s11\" title=\"Part 11 - Supervised Statistical Selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Chi-square, ANOVA, MI</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - SelectKBest\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s12\" title=\"Part 12 - SelectKBest\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>SelectKBest and the benchmark</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - Wrapper Methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s13\" title=\"Part 13 - Wrapper Methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Wrapper methods (RFE)</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - Embedded Methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s14\" title=\"Part 14 - Embedded Methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Embedded methods</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 - Filter vs Wrapper vs Embedded\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s15\" title=\"Part 15 - Filter vs Wrapper vs Embedded\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Filter vs wrapper vs embedded</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 - Data Leakage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s16\" title=\"Part 16 - Data Leakage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Data leakage and pipelines</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 17 - Full Subjective Scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s17\" title=\"Part 17 - Full Subjective Scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>10-mark factory scenario</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 18 - Another Important Scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s18\" title=\"Part 18 - Another Important Scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>500 features, 100 rows</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 19 - Likely Subjective Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s19\" title=\"Part 19 - Likely Subjective Questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Likely questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 20 - How to recognize good and weak answers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s20\" title=\"Part 20 - How to recognize good and weak answers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Weak vs strong answers</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 21 - The entire chapter as one connected story\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s21\" title=\"Part 21 - The entire chapter as one connected story\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>The chapter as one story</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l5\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Lecture 5 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>22 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 5 - Subjective Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Feature Scaling and Feature Selection</h4>\n<p>TEB3123 Machine Learning · Chapter 4 in your lecture slides</p>\n<p>Lecture 5 is actually about two completely different problems that can affect machine-learning performance.</p>\n<p>Imagine you've completed the previous lectures. You have collected data, cleaned missing values, encoded categorical variables and engineered useful features.</p>\n<p>But your model still performs poorly.</p>\n<p>There are two possible explanations:</p>\n<p>Problem 1 - Your features are measured on incompatible numerical scales.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age                 25\nMonthly Income      85000\nYears of Experience 3\n</code></pre>\n<p>Problem 2 - Your dataset contains too many unnecessary or redundant features.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age\nMonthly Income\nYears of Experience\nStudent ID\nFavourite Colour\nRandom Number\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>This chapter addresses those problems separately.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Fundamental question</th>\n<th>What changes?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Scaling</td>\n<td>Are numerical magnitudes distorting how the algorithm learns?</td>\n<td>Feature values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Selection</td>\n<td>Are all these features actually useful for prediction?</td>\n<td>Number of features</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture covers three major scaling techniques and three families of feature-selection methods.</p>\n<p>For a subjective exam, we're going beyond definitions. You need to understand the mathematical mechanism, when the method is appropriate, why another method might be inferior, and what conclusions can reasonably be drawn from the results.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - How to answer subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The best approach for most 5-10 mark questions is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nIdentify\n\\rightarrow Explain\n\\rightarrow Justify\n\\rightarrow Evaluate\n}$$</div>\n<p>For example, suppose the examiner asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A bank has customer-income data containing extreme outliers. Recommend an appropriate feature-scaling method.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use RobustScaler because it handles outliers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer would be:</p>\n<p>RobustScaler is appropriate to consider because the dataset contains extreme income values that can distort statistics such as the mean, standard deviation, minimum and maximum. RobustScaler transforms numerical features using the median and interquartile range, which are less sensitive to outliers. Consequently, ordinary income observations are less likely to become compressed by a small number of extreme values. However, the outliers remain in the dataset, and the method should still be assessed using the model's validation performance.</p>\n<p>Notice the difference: the stronger answer explains what, why, how and limitations.</p>\n<p>That is the standard we'll aim for.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - Feature Scaling</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_space3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Why scaling changes distances</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · The same customers in raw units and in z-scores. Raw salary stretches one axis so far that age and experience vanish - and the nearest neighbour changes.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>1. Why does feature scaling matter?</h4>\n<p>Imagine predicting customer loan default using KNN.</p>\n<p>Two customers have:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Customer A</th>\n<th>Customer B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Age</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Income (RM)</td>\n<td>80,000</td>\n<td>85,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experience (years)</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>KNN calculates numerical distance to find similar customers.</p>\n<p>Using Euclidean distance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d(A,B)=\\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^{n}(x_{Aj}-x_{Bj})^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d=\\sqrt{(25-30)^2+(80000-85000)^2+(3-5)^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{25+25,000,000+4}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{d\\approx5000}$$</div>\n<p>Look carefully at the squared differences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age contributes 25.</li>\n<li>Income contributes 25,000,000.</li>\n<li>Experience contributes 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Income numerically dominates the calculation.</p>\n<p>But does this prove income is a million times more relevant than age?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>It only proves that income happens to use much larger numerical values.</p>\n<p>Now imagine changing income from ringgit to thousands of ringgit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM80,000\\rightarrow80$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM85,000\\rightarrow85$$</div>\n<p>The underlying customers haven't changed at all.</p>\n<p>Yet the numerical distance changes dramatically.</p>\n<p>This exposes the fundamental problem:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>An algorithm should not mistake the units of measurement for the actual predictive importance of a feature.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Feature scaling transforms numerical values to make features more comparable.</p>\n<p>The distance-dominance example and algorithm sensitivity are central to slides 11-14.</p>\n<h4>2. Does every ML algorithm need scaling?</h4>\n<p>No. It depends on how the algorithm uses features.</p>\n<p>Consider two algorithms.</p>\n<h5>KNN</h5>\n<p>KNN asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which observations are numerically closest?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Distance is fundamental to its operation.</p>\n<p>Therefore, feature magnitude matters.</p>\n<h5>Decision Tree</h5>\n<p>A Decision Tree might ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Income&gt;RM80,000?$$</div>\n<p>If income is converted to thousands of ringgit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Income&gt;80?$$</div>\n<p>The underlying split can represent the same decision.</p>\n<p>This is why the lecture treats tree-based models as comparatively insensitive to scaling.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Algorithm</th>\n<th>Scaling importance</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Distance determines neighbours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>K-Means</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Clustering is distance-based</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVM</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Geometry and kernel distances matter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PCA</td>\n<td>Often high</td>\n<td>Large-variance features can dominate components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neural Networks</td>\n<td>Often beneficial</td>\n<td>Can improve optimization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logistic Regression</td>\n<td>Often beneficial</td>\n<td>Can improve optimization and regularization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision Tree</td>\n<td>Generally low</td>\n<td>Uses split thresholds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n<td>Generally low</td>\n<td>Ensemble of trees</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain why scaling is important for KNN but generally unnecessary for Random Forest. (5 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>KNN is a distance-based algorithm that identifies similar observations using numerical distance measures. When features have significantly different ranges, variables with larger magnitudes can dominate the distance calculation even if they are not more predictive. Feature scaling therefore places numerical variables on comparable scales. In contrast, Random Forest uses decision-tree splits that compare individual features against thresholds. These splits are generally unaffected by consistent changes in numerical scale, so Random Forest typically does not require scaling.</p>\n<p>The key is explaining the mechanism, not simply listing which models need scaling.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - StandardScaler</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>3. StandardScaler: how far is a value from the average?</h4>\n<p>The lecture introduces StandardScaler through Z-score standardization.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x=\\text{original observation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\mu=\\text{feature mean}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma=\\text{feature standard deviation}$$</div>\n<p>The result expresses how far a value is from the mean in standard-deviation units.</p>\n<h5>Intuitive example</h5>\n<p>Suppose an examination has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Mean=50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$StandardDeviation=10$$</div>\n<p>A student scores 70.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=\\frac{70-50}{10}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=2}$$</div>\n<h5>What does that mean?</h5>\n<p>The student scored two standard deviations above the average.</p>\n<p>Now consider a student scoring 40.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=\\frac{40-50}{10}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=-1}$$</div>\n<p>Meaning:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The student scored one standard deviation below average.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>How to interpret Z-scores</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Z-score</th>\n<th>Interpretation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>At the mean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>+1</td>\n<td>One standard deviation above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>+2</td>\n<td>Two standard deviations above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-1</td>\n<td>One standard deviation below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-2</td>\n<td>Two standard deviations below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture develops these ideas on slides 17-20.</p>\n<h4>4. Why does StandardScaler work?</h4>\n<p>Imagine two features:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age:    mean 30, SD 10\nIncome: mean 80000, SD 20000\n</code></pre>\n<p>A customer has:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age    = 40\nIncome = 100000\n</code></pre>\n<p>After standardization:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Age_z=\\frac{40-30}{10}=1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Income_z=\\frac{100000-80000}{20000}=1$$</div>\n<p>Both become:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div>\n<p>This does not mean age and income are equally important.</p>\n<p>It means the customer is one standard deviation above the average in both features.</p>\n<p>The transformation makes comparisons less dependent on their original measurement units.</p>\n<p>For nonconstant training features, StandardScaler produces approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Mean=0,\\quad StandardDeviation=1$$</div>\n<h5>Major limitation</h5>\n<p>StandardScaler uses the mean and standard deviation.</p>\n<p>Both can be influenced by outliers.</p>\n<p>If most people earn RM50,000 but one person earns RM10 million, the extreme salary can substantially change the mean and standard deviation.</p>\n<p>Therefore, StandardScaler does not make the data immune to outliers.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - MinMaxScaler</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>5. MinMaxScaler: where does a value lie within its range?</h4>\n<p>The formula is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}}}$$</div>\n<p>Its usual goal is to transform the training range into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[0,1]$$</div>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Minimum=20$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Maximum=60$$</div>\n<p>and the observed value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x=30$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{30-20}{60-20}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac{10}{40}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=0.25}$$</div>\n<p>The observation lies 25% of the way from the minimum to the maximum.</p>\n<h5>Meaning of the endpoints</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Minimum\\rightarrow0$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Maximum\\rightarrow1$$</div>\n<p>Values between those endpoints are proportionally positioned between 0 and 1.</p>\n<h5>Why is this useful?</h5>\n<p>Suppose an algorithm benefits from inputs represented within a bounded range.</p>\n<p>Min-Max scaling preserves the relative ordering and proportional spacing of observations within each feature, while adjusting their numerical range.</p>\n<p>The lecture presents this method and its Python implementation on slides 21-23.</p>\n<h4>6. Why can MinMaxScaler perform poorly with outliers?</h4>\n<p>The lecture uses video-view data.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X=[1,5,25,300,1295,19000]$$</div>\n<p>The maximum is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$19000$$</div>\n<p>Now scale 1,295:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{1295-1}{19000-1}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'\\approx0.0681}$$</div>\n<p>That means a video with 1,295 views becomes approximately 0.068 on the scaled range.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because the video with 19,000 views determines the maximum.</p>\n<p>Most ordinary observations become squeezed toward zero.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Views</th>\n<th>Approximate Min-Max value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0.0000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0.0002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>0.0013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>300</td>\n<td>0.0157</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,295</td>\n<td>0.0681</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19,000</td>\n<td>1.0000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is the central lesson of the video-view comparison on slide 27.</p>\n<h5>The deeper lesson</h5>\n<p>MinMaxScaler is not wrong because outliers exist.</p>\n<p>It simply uses the extremes as reference points.</p>\n<p>If those extreme values are unrepresentative of ordinary observations, the transformed representation may become less useful for the chosen model.</p>\n<p>Additional technical note: The [0,1] range is based on the data used to fit the scaler. A future value outside that training range may transform to a number below 0 or above 1.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - RobustScaler</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>7. RobustScaler: what if the average is misleading?</h4>\n<p>Imagine five monthly incomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[2000,3000,4000,5000,100000]$$</div>\n<p>The mean is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\mu=\\frac{2000+3000+4000+5000+100000}{5}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\mu=22800}$$</div>\n<p>But four of the five people earn RM5,000 or less.</p>\n<p>The mean of RM22,800 is not representative of a typical person in this small dataset.</p>\n<p>The median is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{4000}$$</div>\n<p>It is less sensitive to the one extreme income.</p>\n<p>RobustScaler takes advantage of this.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nx'=\\frac{x-\\text{Median}(X)}{IQR(X)}\n}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IQR=Q_3-Q_1$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Q_1=\\text{25th percentile}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Q_3=\\text{75th percentile}$$</div>\n<h5>The intuition</h5>\n<p>StandardScaler asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How far is this observation from the mean, measured in standard deviations?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>RobustScaler asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How far is this observation from the median, measured using the spread of the middle 50%?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This matters because the median and IQR are less sensitive to extreme values.</p>\n<h4>8. Worked RobustScaler calculation</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Q_1=10,\\quad Median=20,\\quad Q_3=30$$</div>\n<p>For:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x=25$$</div>\n<p>First calculate the IQR:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IQR=30-10=20$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{25-20}{20}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=0.25}$$</div>\n<p>Notice the transformed value is not necessarily between zero and one.</p>\n<p>Unlike MinMaxScaler, RobustScaler does not guarantee a bounded output.</p>\n<h5>Important limitation</h5>\n<p>RobustScaler does not remove outliers.</p>\n<p>It changes the reference statistics used for scaling.</p>\n<p>An extremely large observation may remain extremely large after scaling.</p>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes this difference in its RobustScaler explanation and three-scaler comparison on slides 24-28.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Comparing the three scalers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_scalers\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Three scalers, one outlier</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Animated · The lecture&#x27;s video views under all three scalers. Make the viral video bigger and watch which scaler lets it squash the ordinary videos.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>9. The comparison examiners love</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>StandardScaler</th>\n<th>MinMaxScaler</th>\n<th>RobustScaler</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference statistics</td>\n<td>Mean, SD</td>\n<td>Minimum, maximum</td>\n<td>Median, IQR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical transformed training scale</td>\n<td>Mean 0, SD 1</td>\n<td>0-1</td>\n<td>Median 0, IQR 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensitivity to outliers</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Very sensitive</td>\n<td>Less sensitive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can produce negative values?</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Not within training range</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Removes outliers?</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler and RobustScaler and discuss their suitability for data containing outliers. (8 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>StandardScaler standardizes each numerical feature by subtracting its mean and dividing by its standard deviation, producing a training distribution centered around zero with unit standard deviation. It is useful for algorithms sensitive to feature magnitude, but its statistics may be influenced by extreme values.</p>\n<p>MinMaxScaler uses the observed minimum and maximum to map the training data into a predefined range, usually zero to one. However, it is highly sensitive to extreme values because the minimum and maximum determine the transformation. A large outlier may cause ordinary observations to become compressed into a small portion of the transformed range.</p>\n<p>RobustScaler instead uses the median and interquartile range. These statistics are less sensitive to extreme values, making RobustScaler suitable to consider for heavily skewed or outlier-prone data. Nevertheless, it does not remove outliers, and the preferred scaler ultimately depends on the data, learning algorithm and validation performance.</p>\n<p>The reasoning to remember:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{StandardScaler:\\ Mean+SD}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{MinMaxScaler:\\ Min+Max}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{RobustScaler:\\ Median+IQR}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - Feature Selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>10. What if the problem isn't the scale, but the features themselves?</h4>\n<p>Suppose you're predicting whether a student will pass an examination.</p>\n<p>Your dataset has:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Attendance\nStudy Hours\nPrevious GPA\nAssignment Scores\nStudent ID\nFavourite Colour\nShoe Size\nPrevious GPA converted into percentage\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are eight features, but are all eight useful?</p>\n<p>Consider three different situations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Useful feature:</strong> Attendance may help predict examination performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Irrelevant feature:</strong> Shoe size may have little meaningful relationship with examination performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Redundant feature:</strong> Previous GPA and its equivalent percentage representation contain nearly the same information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Feature selection attempts to identify and retain the most informative features while removing unnecessary ones.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nOriginal\\ Features\n\\rightarrow\nUseful\\ Subset\n}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture explains that this can reduce computational costs, irrelevant information, redundancy and the risk of overfitting.</p>\n<h5>Critical distinction</h5>\n<p>Feature selection is not the same as feature engineering.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Engineering</td>\n<td>Create/transform features</td>\n<td>Debt ÷ Income</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Selection</td>\n<td>Keep or remove original features</td>\n<td>Remove Shoe Size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Extraction</td>\n<td>Construct new representation dimensions</td>\n<td>PCA components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Scaling</td>\n<td>Change feature magnitudes</td>\n<td>RM80,000 → standardized value</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Differentiate feature scaling and feature selection. (4 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Feature scaling transforms the numerical values of features to make their magnitudes more comparable, particularly for algorithms that depend on distance or numerical optimization. Feature selection instead identifies and retains informative variables while removing irrelevant or redundant ones. Scaling generally preserves the number of original features, whereas selection reduces the number of input variables. Both are preprocessing techniques, but they address different problems affecting model performance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - Curse of Dimensionality</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>11. Why can having too many features be harmful?</h4>\n<p>This is the deeper reason feature selection matters.</p>\n<p>Imagine you're trying to identify similar customers.</p>\n<p>Using only two features:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Age,\\ Income$$</div>\n<p>you may find plenty of customers with similar values.</p>\n<p>Now add:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age\nIncome\nEducation\nLocation\nEmployment\nShopping Frequency\nPurchase History\nDevice Type\nWebsite Activity\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>The number of possible feature combinations becomes enormous.</p>\n<p>Suppose each feature can take 10 possible values.</p>\n<p>With two features:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10^2=100$$</div>\n<p>With ten features:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10^{10}$$</div>\n<p>possible combinations.</p>\n<p>With limited data, observations become increasingly sparse in the high-dimensional space.</p>\n<p>This is the intuition behind the curse of dimensionality, which the lecture introduces on page 32.</p>\n<h5>Why is this problematic?</h5>\n<p>A model may experience greater computational demands, difficulty identifying reliable patterns and increased overfitting risk.</p>\n<p>For distance-based algorithms such as KNN, the concept of nearby observations may also become less informative when many irrelevant dimensions are included.</p>\n<p>But here's an important distinction:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Having many features isn't automatically bad. Having too many features relative to their useful information, the sample size and the chosen model is the problem.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A genuinely informative additional feature could improve performance.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the goal is not to minimize the number of features at all costs.</p>\n<p>It is to retain a useful, generalizable representation.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - The three feature-selection approaches</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_families\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Filter methods score features once; wrappers refit a model in a loop; embedded methods get importance while training.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture divides feature selection into three families:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nFilter,\\ Wrapper,\\ Embedded\n}$$</div>\n<p>These are among the most important topics for your subjective exam.</p>\n<h4>12. Understand all three intuitively</h4>\n<p>Imagine hiring a football team.</p>\n<h5>Filter method</h5>\n<p>You examine applicants using predefined statistics:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Fitness score\nSpeed\nExperience\n</code></pre>\n<p>You select those above certain thresholds.</p>\n<p>You do not repeatedly assemble and test entire teams.</p>\n<h5>Wrapper method</h5>\n<p>You repeatedly assemble different teams, evaluate their performance and remove weaker contributors.</p>\n<p>Selection depends on how the chosen team performs.</p>\n<h5>Embedded method</h5>\n<p>During the training of the team, a system evaluates player contributions and identifies useful players as part of that process.</p>\n<p>Now translate that into machine learning.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>How it selects features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter</td>\n<td>Statistical scores or thresholds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrapper</td>\n<td>Repeatedly fitting models and eliminating/testing subsets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Embedded</td>\n<td>Information obtained from model training itself</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The slides introduce this exact taxonomy on pages 31-33.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - Filter Methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>13. What is a filter method?</h4>\n<p>A filter method chooses features using statistical criteria rather than repeatedly training the final predictive model on candidate subsets.</p>\n<p>The lecture discusses three main forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Threshold-based filtering</li>\n<li>Variance-based filtering</li>\n<li>Supervised statistical scoring</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each answers a different question.</p>\n<h4>14. Threshold-based filtering</h4>\n<p>Imagine a dataset containing 10,000 text documents.</p>\n<p>A term appears only once:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;qwerty123&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another appears in almost every document:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;the&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Would either be especially useful for distinguishing document categories?</p>\n<p>Possibly not.</p>\n<p>A threshold-based method can filter terms based on document frequency.</p>\n<p>The lecture's example uses:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(\n    min_df=0.10,\n    max_df=0.85,\n    max_features=2000\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Interpret:</p>\n<h5>min_df=0.10</h5>\n<p>Exclude words appearing in fewer than 10% of the documents.</p>\n<h5>max_df=0.85</h5>\n<p>Exclude words appearing in more than 85% of the documents.</p>\n<h5>max_features=2000</h5>\n<p>Restrict the generated vocabulary to at most 2,000 selected features.</p>\n<p>This is the example from pages 35-36.</p>\n<h5>But why can filtering rare words be dangerous?</h5>\n<p>Imagine detecting fraudulent emails.</p>\n<p>The phrase:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;verify_unusual_transfer&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>might be rare but highly indicative of suspicious content.</p>\n<p>If your frequency threshold is too aggressive, you may remove useful information.</p>\n<p>The deeper lesson:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Frequency is not the same as predictive relevance.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>15. VarianceThreshold - remove features that barely change</h4>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Customer</th>\n<th>Feature A</th>\n<th>Feature B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>11</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Feature A is constant.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$A=[1,1,1,1]$$</div>\n<p>Its variance is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Var(A)=0}$$</div>\n<p>Feature B varies.</p>\n<p>Therefore, a zero-variance threshold would remove Feature A.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because it cannot distinguish one training observation from another.</p>\n<p>The lecture demonstrates this using six genetic markers, selecting Gen 1, Gen 3 and Gen 5 when applying a variance threshold of 0.15 on pages 37-38.</p>\n<h5>Core code</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import VarianceThreshold\n\nselector = VarianceThreshold(threshold=0.15)\n\nX_train_selected = selector.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_test_selected = selector.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The crucial point:</p>\n<p>VarianceThreshold does not need target labels.</p>\n<p>It examines:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X$$</div>\n<p>without evaluating the relationship with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$y$$</div>\n<p>This makes it an unsupervised filter method.</p>\n<h4>16. Why low variance doesn't necessarily mean useless</h4>\n<p>This is an excellent higher-level exam trap.</p>\n<p>Suppose we're predicting fraud.</p>\n<p>A binary feature records whether a transaction contains a rare suspicious pattern.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">99% of transactions → 0\n1% of transactions  → 1\n</code></pre>\n<p>This feature has low variance.</p>\n<p>For a binary variable with proportion $p$ of ones:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Var(X)=p(1-p)$$</div>\n<p>Here:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$p=0.01$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Var(X)=0.01(0.99)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Var(X)=0.0099}$$</div>\n<p>If the variance threshold is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.15$$</div>\n<p>this feature will be removed.</p>\n<p>But what if the rare positive pattern is strongly associated with fraud?</p>\n<p>The feature may be extremely valuable despite its low variance.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>VarianceThreshold evaluates how much a feature varies, not whether that variation is useful for predicting the target.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain one limitation of variance-based feature selection. (4 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Variance-based selection removes features whose variance falls below a specified threshold. This is useful for eliminating constant or near-constant variables that contribute little variation across observations. However, the method does not consider the relationship between the feature and the target variable. Consequently, a low-variance feature may be removed even when its rare values are highly predictive, such as an unusual transaction indicator used in fraud detection. Therefore, variance-based selection should not automatically be treated as a measure of predictive relevance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - Supervised Statistical Selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>17. Why do statistical tests improve on simple variance filtering?</h4>\n<p>VarianceThreshold asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does this feature change?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A supervised statistical test asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does this feature contain information about the outcome I'm trying to predict?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's a more relevant question for supervised learning.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces three methods:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Main purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chi-Square ($\\chi^2$)</td>\n<td>Evaluate association with a categorical target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANOVA F-Test</td>\n<td>Evaluate differences in a numerical feature across target classes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutual Information</td>\n<td>Measure statistical dependence between feature and target</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These methods are discussed on pages 39-41.</p>\n<h4>18. Chi-Square</h4>\n<p>Suppose you're predicting loan default.</p>\n<p>You have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Owns_House\nLoan_Default\n</code></pre>\n<p>You want to know whether home ownership and loan default are statistically associated.</p>\n<p>Chi-Square measures how much the observed relationship differs from what would be expected under independence.</p>\n<p>If the observed pattern differs substantially from independence, the feature may have a stronger association with the target.</p>\n<h5>Important distinction</h5>\n<p>Chi-Square does not establish causality.</p>\n<p>It establishes evidence of statistical association under its assumptions.</p>\n<p>In scikit-learn, the chi2 scoring function also requires non-negative feature inputs.</p>\n<p>That implementation constraint becomes important when constructing pipelines.</p>\n<h4>19. ANOVA F-Test</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Feature = Income\nTarget = Default / No Default\n</code></pre>\n<p>We want to know whether income differs meaningfully between the two target classes.</p>\n<p>ANOVA compares variation between class groups relative to variation within those groups.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Are the average income levels of defaulters and non-defaulters sufficiently different relative to the variation inside each group?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If yes, income may be informative for classification.</p>\n<h4>20. Mutual Information</h4>\n<p>Mutual Information takes a more general approach.</p>\n<p>It asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How much does knowing feature X reduce uncertainty about target Y?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If knowing X provides useful information about Y, mutual information is positive.</p>\n<p>A key advantage is that mutual information can identify nonlinear statistical dependencies that simple linear correlation may miss.</p>\n<p><strong>Compare the three</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Main focus</th>\n<th>Important caution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chi-Square</td>\n<td>Association with categorical targets using suitable non-negative inputs</td>\n<td>Non-negative input requirement in scikit-learn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANOVA F-Test</td>\n<td>Class-dependent differences in continuous features</td>\n<td>Does not generally capture arbitrary nonlinear patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutual Information</td>\n<td>Statistical dependency</td>\n<td>Estimation may be sensitive to data/sample characteristics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5>Why are these called univariate methods?</h5>\n<p>Because standard SelectKBest-style scoring evaluates each feature individually.</p>\n<p>This introduces a limitation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1\\text{ alone may be weak}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_2\\text{ alone may be weak}$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1\\times X_2$$</div>\n<p>might be strongly predictive.</p>\n<p>Individual statistical scores could miss such interactions.</p>\n<p>That's one reason the lecture introduces wrapper methods afterward.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - SelectKBest</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>21. How does SelectKBest work?</h4>\n<p>Suppose your dataset contains 30 features.</p>\n<p>You calculate a statistical score for each feature.</p>\n<p>Then rank them:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Feature 1 → Score 35\nFeature 2 → Score 28\nFeature 3 → Score 21\n...\nFeature 30 → Score 1\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you specify:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$k=15$$</div>\n<p>the highest-scoring 15 features are retained.</p>\n<p>The lecture applies this approach to the Wisconsin Breast Cancer dataset.</p>\n<h5>The dataset</h5>\n<p>The benchmark contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>569 observations</li>\n<li>30 numerical features</li>\n<li>Two target classes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide uses Chi-Square-based selection to reduce 30 features to 15.</p>\n<h5>Core code</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, chi2\n\nselector = SelectKBest(\n    score_func=chi2,\n    k=15\n)\n\nX_selected = selector.fit_transform(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Assume the input features are non-negative, as required by chi2.</p>\n<h5>Why does y_train appear?</h5>\n<p>Because Chi-Square is supervised feature selection.</p>\n<p>It evaluates features in relation to the known target labels.</p>\n<p>Compare this with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">VarianceThreshold()\n</code></pre>\n<p>which does not need target labels.</p>\n<h4>22. Interpreting the lecture's experimental results</h4>\n<p>The slides compare Logistic Regression trained using all 30 features against a model using 15 selected features.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>All features</th>\n<th>Selected features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of features</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reported accuracy</td>\n<td>95.09%</td>\n<td>95.26%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The selected-feature model achieves a small reported improvement:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$95.26-95.09$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{0.17\\ percentage\\ points}$$</div>\n<p>Source: pages 42-45.</p>\n<h5>What should you conclude?</h5>\n<p>The result illustrates that feature selection can reduce the number of variables without necessarily harming predictive performance.</p>\n<p>However, you should not automatically conclude:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Selecting 15 features is always better than using all 30.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because the improvement is small.</p>\n<p>You would need appropriate repeated evaluation or other evidence to determine whether it represents a reliable improvement rather than sampling variation.</p>\n<p>Also, any feature selection must be performed within the training process to avoid leakage into validation/test results.</p>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A model achieves 95.09% accuracy using 30 features and 95.26% using 15 selected features. Evaluate the results. (6 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The results show that reducing the feature count by 50% produced a slight reported increase in classification accuracy from 95.09% to 95.26%. This demonstrates that retaining every original feature is not necessarily beneficial, since some features may be redundant, irrelevant or noisy. A reduced feature set may also lower computational costs and improve model simplicity. However, the improvement is only 0.17 percentage points and does not by itself establish that the reduced model generalizes better. Further validation is required, and feature selection must be performed without allowing test-set information to influence the training procedure.</p>\n<p>This is a stronger answer than simply stating that the selected model is better because 95.26% exceeds 95.09%.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - Wrapper Methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_rfe\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Recursive feature elimination</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Animated · Recursive feature elimination on the Wisconsin data, one refit and one dropped feature at a time, then compared with chi-square.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>23. Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE)</h4>\n<p>Filter methods evaluate features using predefined statistics.</p>\n<p>But suppose two features are useful only when they are considered together.</p>\n<p>Or suppose two individually useful features are highly redundant.</p>\n<p>A wrapper method tries to evaluate feature usefulness through an actual predictive model.</p>\n<p>The lecture uses Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE).</p>\n<h5>RFE process</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nTrain\n\\rightarrow\nRank\n\\rightarrow\nRemove\n\\rightarrow\nRepeat\n}$$</div>\n<p>Imagine starting with 10 features.</p>\n<p>Train a model.</p>\n<p>Identify the weakest feature according to the fitted estimator.</p>\n<p>Remove it.</p>\n<p>Train again using the remaining nine.</p>\n<p>Repeat until only five features remain.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10\\rightarrow9\\rightarrow8\\rightarrow7\\rightarrow6\\rightarrow5$$</div>\n<p>The procedure is illustrated on pages 47-49.</p>\n<h5>Core code</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import RFE\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\nmodel = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)\n\nselector = RFE(\n    estimator=model,\n    n_features_to_select=5,\n    step=1\n)\n\nselector.fit(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>estimator=model specifies the model used to rank features.</li>\n<li>n_features_to_select=5 means retain five features.</li>\n<li>step=1 removes one feature per iteration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Why can RFE be useful?</h5>\n<p>It evaluates features using a predictive estimator rather than a simple standalone statistic.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the usefulness of a feature may depend on the other features currently retained.</p>\n<h5>Why can RFE be expensive?</h5>\n<p>Because it repeatedly trains the estimator.</p>\n<p>For 10,000 text features, removing one feature per iteration can be extremely expensive.</p>\n<h5>Important limitation</h5>\n<p>RFE does not test every possible subset of features.</p>\n<p>It follows a recursive elimination strategy.</p>\n<p>Its decisions also depend on the chosen estimator. A linear estimator, for example, does not automatically discover arbitrary nonlinear interactions.</p>\n<h4>24. Why did Chi-Square and RFE choose different features?</h4>\n<p>The lecture states that when both methods select 15 features, only eight selected features overlap.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because they assess feature usefulness differently.</p>\n<p>Chi-Square evaluates individual statistical associations between each feature and the target.</p>\n<p>RFE repeatedly uses a predictive estimator to rank and eliminate features within the currently retained subset.</p>\n<p>Therefore, a feature can score highly individually but become redundant when another related feature is already included.</p>\n<p>This gives you an important subjective point:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Different selection methods may choose different features because they optimize different criteria and use different definitions of usefulness.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare filter methods and wrapper methods. (6 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Filter methods select features using statistical criteria such as variance, correlation, Chi-Square or mutual information, without repeatedly fitting the final predictive estimator. They are generally computationally efficient and useful for initially reducing large feature sets. However, univariate filters may fail to capture relationships involving combinations of features. Wrapper methods, such as Recursive Feature Elimination, repeatedly fit a predictive model and assess feature importance in the context of the retained subset. This makes selection more closely tied to the chosen model, but it increases computational cost. Therefore, filter methods are useful for efficient preliminary screening, while wrapper methods may be suitable when model-dependent feature assessment is worth the additional computation.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - Embedded Methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>25. What makes embedded methods different?</h4>\n<p>Embedded methods obtain information about feature relevance as part of a model's fitting process.</p>\n<p>The lecture uses Random Forest as its main example.</p>\n<p>A Random Forest builds many Decision Trees.</p>\n<p>Each tree makes decisions using feature-based splits.</p>\n<p>Some features produce more useful splits than others.</p>\n<p>The Random Forest can aggregate this information into feature-importance scores.</p>\n<h5>Core code</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n\nmodel = RandomForestClassifier(random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nimportance = model.feature_importances_\n</code></pre>\n<p>The resulting array contains one importance score for each input feature.</p>\n<p>The lecture presents this approach on pages 51-53.</p>\n<h4>26. What does Random Forest feature importance actually mean?</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Importance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Age</td>\n<td>0.40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Income</td>\n<td>0.25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experience</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shoe Size</td>\n<td>0.15</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The model assigned Age the highest importance under its importance measure.</p>\n<p>But be very careful.</p>\n<p>Does this prove that Age causes the outcome?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>It means Age contributed strongly to the fitted model's decision-making according to that importance calculation.</p>\n<p>This is the distinction between:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Predictive\\ Importance}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Causality}$$</div>\n<p>A variable can predict an outcome without causing it.</p>\n<p>For example, umbrella sales may help predict rainy weather, but buying an umbrella does not cause rain.</p>\n<h5>Additional evaluation caution</h5>\n<p>Random Forest's impurity-based importance may be affected by correlated features and feature characteristics. Consequently, importance scores should not automatically be interpreted as a definitive ranking of real-world causal significance.</p>\n<p>Also, feature-importance ranking alone does not necessarily remove features. You would still need to choose a selection rule or threshold if your goal is to construct a smaller feature set.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 - Filter vs Wrapper vs Embedded</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>27. The most important comparison table</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Filter</th>\n<th>Wrapper</th>\n<th>Embedded</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main mechanism</td>\n<td>Statistical criteria</td>\n<td>Repeated model fitting</td>\n<td>Model-based importance during fitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computational cost</td>\n<td>Generally low</td>\n<td>Generally high</td>\n<td>Often moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses target labels?</td>\n<td>Sometimes</td>\n<td>Usually</td>\n<td>Usually</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interaction handling</td>\n<td>Limited for univariate filters</td>\n<td>Depends on estimator</td>\n<td>Depends on estimator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>VarianceThreshold, Chi-Square</td>\n<td>RFE</td>\n<td>Random Forest importance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Major advantage</td>\n<td>Speed</td>\n<td>Model-dependent assessment</td>\n<td>Integrated with model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Major limitation</td>\n<td>May ignore feature interactions</td>\n<td>Computationally expensive</td>\n<td>Importance depends on fitted model</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5>How to decide in a scenario</h5>\n<p>Suppose you have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10,000\\ text\\ features$$</div>\n<p>and limited computing resources.</p>\n<p>Would you immediately apply RFE?</p>\n<p>Probably not.</p>\n<p>A sensible workflow is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10,000\\ Features$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Filter\\ Methods$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Smaller\\ Candidate\\ Set$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Optional\\ Wrapper/Embedded\\ Assessment$$</div>\n<p>The lecture's final practice scenarios specifically raise this issue.</p>\n<p>The deeper lesson:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Selection methods trade off computational efficiency against how closely they evaluate the actual predictive model.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 - Data Leakage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_cv_pipeline\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Scaling and selection learn from data, so they must be refitted on the training part of every fold.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>28. Why feature scaling and selection must use training data only</h4>\n<p>This is arguably the most important practical principle in the chapter.</p>\n<p>Recall Lecture 2:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Don't let test data influence model development.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The same principle applies here.</p>\n<h5>Scaling learns parameters</h5>\n<p>For example, StandardScaler learns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Mean,\\ StandardDeviation$$</div>\n<p>MinMaxScaler learns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Minimum,\\ Maximum$$</div>\n<p>RobustScaler learns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Median,\\ IQR$$</div>\n<h5>Feature selection also learns from data</h5>\n<p>For example, SelectKBest learns feature scores using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_{train},y_{train}$$</div>\n<p>RFE learns a feature ranking using a trained estimator.</p>\n<p>If you use the full dataset to learn these transformations before splitting it, information from the held-out test observations influences model preparation.</p>\n<p>That is data leakage.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly warns against this on page 54.</p>\n<h4>29. The correct workflow</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nTrain/Test\\ Split\n\\rightarrow\nFit\\ Scaling\\ on\\ Train\n\\rightarrow\nFit\\ Selection\\ on\\ Train\n\\rightarrow\nTrain\\ Model\n\\rightarrow\nEvaluate\\ Test\n}$$</div>\n<p>The test set must use the transformations already learned from training data.</p>\n<h5>Core pipeline</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler\nfrom sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, chi2\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\npipeline = Pipeline([\n    (&quot;scaler&quot;, MinMaxScaler()),\n    (&quot;selector&quot;, SelectKBest(chi2, k=15)),\n    (&quot;model&quot;, LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000))\n])\n\npipeline.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\naccuracy = pipeline.score(X_test, y_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why this arrangement?</p>\n<p>Pipeline ensures that the sequence of transformations and the final estimator are fitted together.</p>\n<p>During training, the scaler and selector learn from X_train and y_train.</p>\n<p>During testing, the fitted preprocessing transformations are reused.</p>\n<h5>A technical issue to recognize</h5>\n<p>The lecture's pipeline illustration uses StandardScaler before Chi-Square selection.</p>\n<p>In scikit-learn, that combination can cause an error because StandardScaler may produce negative feature values while chi2 requires non-negative inputs.</p>\n<p>The example above uses MinMaxScaler to keep the training features non-negative, assuming ordinary nonconstant finite numerical inputs. Alternatively, a compatible statistical scoring method such as f_classif can be considered for standardized numerical features.</p>\n<p>This is a compatibility issue in the illustrative slide code, not a problem with the underlying pipeline principle.</p>\n<h5>Another important point</h5>\n<p>If you use cross-validation, scaling and feature selection should be fitted inside each training fold.</p>\n<p>Otherwise, information from the validation folds can influence preprocessing, causing overly optimistic evaluation results.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 17 - Full Subjective Scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>30. A factory predicts machine failure</h4>\n<p>Suppose the examiner asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A manufacturing company wants to predict equipment failure using temperature, pressure, vibration, machine age and 200 additional sensor variables. Several sensors have large numerical ranges, some record constant values, and others are highly correlated. Propose an appropriate feature-scaling and selection strategy. (10 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The best approach is to reason through the problem systematically.</p>\n<h5>Step 1 - Identify the objective</h5>\n<p>The task is to predict machine failure.</p>\n<p>If the target is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Failure / No Failure\n</code></pre>\n<p>it is supervised classification.</p>\n<h5>Step 2 - Identify whether scaling is needed</h5>\n<p>Some sensors have very different numerical magnitudes.</p>\n<p>If a distance-based or scale-sensitive classifier is used, these differences may distort its calculations.</p>\n<p>Scaling is therefore appropriate.</p>\n<h5>Step 3 - Choose a scaler</h5>\n<p>Inspect the sensor distributions.</p>\n<p>If there are no major outliers, StandardScaler may be suitable.</p>\n<p>If extreme valid sensor readings substantially distort mean and standard deviation, RobustScaler may be preferable.</p>\n<p>The choice should depend on the data distribution and model.</p>\n<h5>Step 4 - Remove constant features</h5>\n<p>Some sensors always record the same value.</p>\n<p>VarianceThreshold can identify and remove such features because they do not vary across the training observations.</p>\n<h5>Step 5 - Evaluate predictive relevance</h5>\n<p>Statistical methods such as ANOVA or mutual information can help screen features against the failure target.</p>\n<p>But don't assume that a low-variance feature is useless - a rare abnormal sensor signal could be highly predictive of failure.</p>\n<h5>Step 6 - Consider redundancy</h5>\n<p>Some sensors measure similar physical quantities and may be highly correlated.</p>\n<p>A further selection method, such as RFE or Random Forest importance, can help assess which features contribute useful information within a particular estimator.</p>\n<h5>Step 7 - Prevent leakage and evaluate</h5>\n<p>All scaling and selection rules must be fitted on the training data.</p>\n<p>Then assess the selected-feature model using held-out or cross-validation data.</p>\n<p><strong>Complete model answer</strong></p>\n<p>The company should first examine its numerical sensor features and their distributions. Since the variables have substantially different numerical ranges, feature scaling may be necessary, particularly if the selected algorithm depends on distance or numerical optimization. StandardScaler may be suitable for reasonably distributed features, whereas RobustScaler is preferable to consider when extreme sensor readings distort mean-based statistics. Feature selection should then address unnecessary dimensions. VarianceThreshold can remove constant or near-constant sensor variables, while supervised statistical tests can assess associations between sensor features and the known failure labels. Additional model-based techniques such as RFE or Random Forest importance may help identify redundant or less informative variables. However, rare low-variance signals should not be removed blindly because they may indicate abnormal machine conditions. To ensure reliable evaluation, all transformations must be learned from the training data and applied consistently to validation and test observations. The final feature subset should be selected based on predictive performance, computational efficiency and its ability to generalize.</p>\n<p>This answer incorporates application, analysis and evaluation, which are higher levels of Bloom's taxonomy.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 18 - Another Important Scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>31. A dataset has 500 features but only 100 observations</h4>\n<p>Suppose the examiner asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Discuss why feature selection may be necessary and recommend an approach. (8 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should immediately recognize a potential dimensionality problem.</p>\n<p>There are more features than observations.</p>\n<p>This can increase overfitting risk, particularly when many features contain noise or are redundant.</p>\n<p>But don't simply say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Remove 400 features.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That would be arbitrary.</p>\n<p>A better approach:</p>\n<p>Feature selection may be useful because the dataset has a high number of variables relative to its sample size, increasing the risk of fitting noise rather than generalizable patterns. An initial filter method can remove constant variables and screen out irrelevant features efficiently. If the task is supervised, statistical scores can help identify candidate features associated with the target. A wrapper or embedded method may then be used on a smaller candidate set to evaluate feature usefulness in the context of the chosen model. However, the number of retained features should be selected through appropriate validation rather than an arbitrary cutoff, and all selection operations must occur within the training procedure to avoid leakage.</p>\n<p>Notice how the answer doesn't merely name methods.</p>\n<p>It explains why the sequence makes sense.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 19 - Likely Subjective Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are the questions I would prepare most seriously.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What your answer must include</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain feature scaling and why it matters</td>\n<td>Different numerical ranges, distance dominance, algorithm sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler and RobustScaler</td>\n<td>Formulas, reference statistics, outlier behavior, suitability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain the curse of dimensionality</td>\n<td>High-dimensional sparsity, overfitting risk, computational cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Differentiate scaling, selection and extraction</td>\n<td>What changes and what remains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain VarianceThreshold and its limitations</td>\n<td>Variance, unsupervised nature, rare predictive features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare Chi-Square, ANOVA and Mutual Information</td>\n<td>Their statistical relationships with targets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain SelectKBest</td>\n<td>Rank features, keep highest K, supervised scoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain how RFE works</td>\n<td>Fit, rank, eliminate, repeat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare filter, wrapper and embedded methods</td>\n<td>Mechanisms, advantages, limitations, trade-offs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain Random Forest feature importance</td>\n<td>Predictive importance, model dependence, noncausality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discuss feature selection using the breast-cancer benchmark</td>\n<td>30 vs 15, accuracy difference, limitations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain preprocessing leakage and pipelines</td>\n<td>Train-only fitting, validation/test consistency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 20 - How to recognize good and weak answers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the question asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why is RobustScaler suitable for data with outliers?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h5>Definition-level answer</h5>\n<p>RobustScaler uses median and IQR.</p>\n<p>Correct, but incomplete.</p>\n<h5>Understanding-level answer</h5>\n<p>RobustScaler uses median and IQR, which are less sensitive to outliers than mean and standard deviation.</p>\n<p>Better.</p>\n<h5>Application-level answer</h5>\n<p>RobustScaler is suitable to consider for salary data containing a few extremely wealthy individuals because median and IQR are less distorted by extreme salaries.</p>\n<p>Better again.</p>\n<h5>Evaluation-level answer</h5>\n<p>RobustScaler may produce a more useful numerical representation for ordinary salary observations when extreme values distort conventional scaling statistics. However, the outliers remain and the scaling method should not be assumed superior without assessing the downstream model's validation performance.</p>\n<p>That is the kind of answer you should strive to write.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 21 - The entire chapter as one connected story</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Let's reconstruct Lecture 5 from first principles.</p>\n<p>We begin with an ML-ready dataset.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X$$</div>\n<p>But some features have vastly different numerical magnitudes.</p>\n<p>That can distort certain algorithms.</p>\n<p>So we use:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Feature\\ Scaling}$$</div>\n<p>If we want to standardize relative to the mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$StandardScaler$$</div>\n<p>If we want to position data within a numerical range:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$MinMaxScaler$$</div>\n<p>If we want scaling statistics that resist extreme observations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RobustScaler$$</div>\n<p>Now suppose our dataset contains many redundant or irrelevant dimensions.</p>\n<p>We use:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Feature\\ Selection}$$</div>\n<p>If we want computationally efficient statistical screening:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Filter$$</div>\n<p>If we want repeated model-based elimination:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Wrapper$$</div>\n<p>If we want feature-importance information obtained during model training:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Embedded$$</div>\n<p>But none of this matters if we accidentally let test information influence the training procedure.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nSplit\\ First\n\\rightarrow\nFit\\ Preprocessing\\ on\\ Train\n\\rightarrow\nApply\\ Consistently\n\\rightarrow\nEvaluate\n}$$</div>\n<h5>Final Exam Takeaway</h5>\n<p>For Lecture 5, I would memorize the three scaler formulas and the three feature-selection families, but spend most of my time understanding the decisions behind them.</p>\n<p>In almost every subjective scenario, ask yourself:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Is the problem numerical scale or unnecessary dimensions?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What does the proposed method actually measure or transform?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Why is it appropriate for this dataset and algorithm?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>What assumption or limitation could make it inappropriate?</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>How will I verify that it genuinely improves performance without data leakage?</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your strongest answers will not say only:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Use RobustScaler.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>or:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Apply RFE.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>They will explain why that technique is appropriate, which problem it solves, what trade-off it introduces, and how you would validate its usefulness.</p>\n<p>That's the difference between memorizing Lecture 5 and being able to answer it confidently in a subjective examination.</p></div></details></div></details>");
